      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is twice the labeled maximum daily dose. The maximum is 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release metformin and 2,000 mg/day for extended-release. A dose that high raises the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nHere are some options that stay within the label:\n- **Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the immediate-release maximum.\n- **Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals.** This is 2,000 mg/day, a common target dose.\n- **Metformin 1,700 mg PO once or twice daily.** Twice daily is 3,400 mg/day, which is still over the maximum, so I wouldn't use it. Once daily is 1,700 mg/day, which is within range.\n\nIf 1,700 mg was meant as the total daily dose, or if it was a typo (for example 850 mg or 1,000 mg), tell me the dose you want and I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
